\section{Supported built-in objective functions}{

The following functions are available with their current behaviour. All are
formulated as objectives to be minimised.

\describe{
\item{\code{norm2}}{
Sum of squared errors on the original scale:
\deqn{\sum (obs - sim)^2}
Typical use: continuous observations with approximately additive errors.
}

\item{\code{lnorm2}}{
Sum of squared errors on the log scale:
\deqn{\sum (\log(obs + tiny) - \log(sim + tiny))^2}
Arguments: \code{tiny} (default \code{1e-2}), added before the log to avoid
\code{log(0)}. Typical use: positive-valued data with multiplicative (lognormal)
error structure.
}

\item{\code{lnorm3}}{
Log-scale squared error with an estimated multiplicative scaling factor
\eqn{q}. Internally:
\itemize{
\item compute element-wise ratios \code{ratio <- obs/sim};
\item set \code{NaN} ratios to \code{NA};
\item estimate \code{q <- mean(ratio, na.rm = TRUE)};
\item return \eqn{\sum (\log(obs+tiny) - \log(sim+tiny) - \log(q))^2}.
}
Typical use: positive data where an overall multiplicative bias (scale
mismatch) is expected and should not be fully penalised.
}

\item{\code{lnorm4} / \code{lnorm4b}}{
Extensions of \code{lnorm3} that add a penalty term to discourage extreme
values of the scaling factor \eqn{q} (or extreme per-observation ratios).
They rely on the helper \code{rangeq()}:
\itemize{
\item compute \code{ratio <- obs/sim}, estimate \code{q <- mean(ratio, na.rm=TRUE)};
\item compute a penalty using parameters \code{b} and \code{c}:
\deqn{pen = n \cdot (\max(|\log_2(q)|, b)^c - b^c)}
when \code{dump = TRUE} (used by \code{lnorm4}), or
\deqn{pen = \sum (\max(|\log_2(ratio)|, b)^c - b^c)}
when \code{dump = FALSE} (used by \code{lnorm4b}),
where \code{n} is the number of non-missing ratios.
\item add the penalty to the \code{lnorm3}-style objective.
}
Arguments: \code{tiny}, \code{b} (default \code{1}), \code{c} (default \code{2}).
Typical use: log-scale fitting where scale drift must be controlled.
}

\item{\code{pois}}{
Poisson negative log-likelihood (up to constants):
\deqn{-\sum (obs \log(sim) - sim)}
Typical use: counts (or count-like rates) with Poisson observation error.
Note: \code{sim} must be positive where \code{obs > 0}.
}

\item{\code{multinom}}{
A composition (multinomial-like) objective operating on matrices.
Inputs are expected as \code{obs} and \code{sim} matrices with:
\itemize{
\item rows = samples (e.g. time steps, hauls, sites),
\item columns = classes (e.g. age/size bins, categories).
}
Internal steps (high-level):
\itemize{
\item Let \eqn{A} be the number of classes (\code{A <- ncol(sim)}).
\item Rows of \code{sim} that are all zeros (excluding rows that are all \code{NA})
are replaced by \code{1} on that row (interpreted as a uniform prior).
\item Row sums are used to compute proportions:
\eqn{Psim = sim/sum(sim)}, \eqn{Pobs = obs/sum(obs)} (row-wise).
\item Rows with \code{sum(sim) == 0} are set to \code{NA} for numerical convenience.
\item Rows with \code{sum(obs) == 0} are set to \code{NA} (interpreted as “no
proportion data available”).
\item A variance term \code{sigma2} and a small stabiliser \code{tiny} are used to
define an objective that penalises discrepancies between \code{Pobs} and \code{Psim}.
}
Arguments: \code{size} (default \code{20}) and \code{tiny} (default \code{1e-3}).
Interpretation: \code{size} plays the role of an effective sample size (larger
values typically increase the weight of the composition fit).
}

\item{\code{normp} / \code{re}}{
Pure penalty on simulated values:
\deqn{\sum sim^2}
This ignores \code{obs} and can be used as a regulariser, or when \code{sim}
represents a residual vector or deviates already computed upstream.
\code{re} is an alias of \code{normp}.
}

\item{\code{penalty}}{
Scaled quadratic penalty:
\deqn{n \cdot mean(sim^2)}
Arguments: \code{n} (default \code{100}). This assumes a fixed sample size and can
be used to put the penalty on a comparable scale across datasets.
}
}
